her they have come from a <span class="ielts-reading-question-item"><span class="ielts-inline-select-wrap"><strong class="ielts-q-badge">16</strong><select data-qnum="16" name="question_16" class="ielts-inline-select"><option value="">[ 16 ] Tanlang...</option><option value="A. basic outlook">A. basic outlook</option><option value="B. employment opportunities">B. employment opportunities</option><option value="C. wealthy visitors">C. wealthy visitors</option><option value="D. distant country">D. distant country</option><option value="E. primary objective">E. primary objective</option><option value="F. similar advantages">F. similar advantages</option><option value="G. thriving economy">G. thriving economy</option><option value="H. nearby district">H. nearby district</option><option value="I. dense population">I. dense population</option><option value="J. new immigrants">J. new immigrants</option></select></span></span> or have travelled from the other side of India, they have all come in search of things such as improved <span class="ielts-reading-question-item"><span class="ielts-inline-select-wrap"><strong class="ielts-q-badge">17</strong><select data-qnum="17" name="question_17" class="ielts-inline-select"><option value="">[ 17 ] Tanlang...</option><option value="A. basic outlook">A. basic outlook</option><option value="B. employment opportunities">B. employment opportunities</option><option value="C. wealthy visitors">C. wealthy visitors</option><option value="D. distant country">D. distant country</option><option value="E. primary objective">E. primary objective</option><option value="F. similar advantages">F. similar advantages</option><option value="G. thriving economy">G. thriving economy</option><option value="H. nearby district">H. nearby district</option><option value="I. dense population">I. dense population</option><option value="J. new immigrants">J. new immigrants</option></select></span></span>.</p>
<p>A team of researchers led by Professor Ianthi Tsimpli of Cambridge University is collecting data on primary-age schoolchildren in Delhi and other Indian cities. The <span class="ielts-reading-question-item"><span class="ielts-inline-select-wrap"><strong class="ielts-q-badge">18</strong><select data-qnum="18" name="question_18" class="ielts-inline-select"><option value="">[ 18 ] Tanlang...</option><option value="A. basic outlook">A. basic outlook</option><option value="B. employment opportunities">B. employment opportunities</option><option value="C. wealthy visitors">C. wealthy visitors</option><option value="D. distant country">D. distant country</option><option value="E. primary objective">E. primary objective</option><option value="F. similar advantages">F. similar advantages</option><option value="G. thriving economy">G. thriving economy</option><option value="H. nearby district">H. nearby district</option><option value="I. dense population">I. dense population</option><option value="J. new immigrants">J. new immigrants</option></select></span></span> of the research is to discover why multilingual Indian schoolchildren do not experience <span class="ielts-reading-question-item"><span class="ielts-inline-select-wrap"><strong class="ielts-q-badge">19</strong><select data-qnum="19" name="question_19" class="ielts-inline-select"><option value="">[ 19 ] Tanlang...</option><option value="A. basic outlook">A. basic outlook</option><option value="B. employment opportunities">B. employment opportunities</option><option value="C. wealthy visitors">C. wealthy visitors</option><option value="D. distant country">D. distant country</option><option value="E. primary objective">E. primary objective</option><option value="F. similar advantages">F. similar advantages</option><option value="G. thriving economy">G. thriving economy</option><option value="H. nearby district">H. nearby district</option><option value="I. dense population">I. dense population</option><option value=